        "content": "Documents give contradicting answers about the same thing. Other documents, which take no side, may state a fact that makes one of the answers impossible.\n\nQuestion: Who was Mahmud I's father?\n\nAnswer A: Malik Shah I\n  about: Mahmud I\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 2\n  evidence: \"Father Malik Shah I\" [document 5]; \"Malik Shah I and proclaimed sultan at Baghdad by the caliph al-Muqtadi ( r. 1075–1094 ) . Under his notional reign , the empire built by his father and Alp Arslan fragmented .\" [document 4]\n\nAnswer B: Osman III\n  about: Mahmud I\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Şehzade Hasan Şehzade Süleyman Mustafa III Abdul Hamid I Şehzade Mehmed ( son of Ahmed III ) Şehzade Beyazid Şehzade Numan Şehzade Mahmud 18th generation Selim III Mustafa IV Mahmud II Şehzade Abdullah Selim 19th generation Abdulmejid I Abdulaziz Mustafa Efendi 20th generation Murad V Abdul Hamid II Mehmed V Şehzade Ahmed Kemaleddin Şehzade Mehmed Burhaneddin Şehzade Ahmed Nureddin\" [document 2]\n\nThe other documents:\nDocument 1: Mahmud I Mahmud I (, , 2 August 1696 13 December 1754) was the Sultan of the Ottoman Empire from 1730 to 1754. He was born at Edirne Palace, the son of Mustafa II (1664–1703); his mother was Saliha Sabkati Valide Sultan. Mahmud I was the older brother of Osman III (1754–57). On 28 September 1730, Patrona Halil with a small group of fellow Janissaries aroused some of the citizens of Constantinople who opposed the reforms of Ahmet III. Sweeping up more soldiers Halil led the riot to the Topkapı Palace and demanded the death of the grand vizer, Nevşehirli\nDocument 3: Mahmud I of Great Seljuq Nasir ad-Din Mahmud I was the sultan of the Seljuk Empire from 1092 to 1094. He succeeded Malik Shah I as Sultan, but he did not gain control of the empire built by Malik Shah and Alp Arslan. Turkan Khatun, the last wife of Malik Shah, tried to win the throne for her 4 years old son Mahmud, who was proclaimed sultan in Bagdad. The older son of Malik shah, Barkyaruk was proclaimed too, and the armies of the two pretenders meet in Barudjird, near Hamadan. The forces of Barkyaruk won and took the capital\n\nLook in the other documents for a fact that rules one answer out: a date of birth that makes a person too young, for example, or a plain statement that the answer is false. Use only what these documents say, nothing you know from elsewhere. A fact that only makes an answer less likely does not count.\n\nReply with JSON only. If such a fact is there:\n{\"reason\": \"one sentence\", \"impossible\": \"A\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\"}\n\"impossible\" is the letter of the answer that is ruled out. \"quote\" is the sentence stating the fact, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in.\nIf there is none:\n{\"impossible\": null}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
